# Active Block Modifiers — design.md §12, and §9's `register_abm`.
#
# An ABM is a mod's answer to "this node should change on its own": grass
# spreads onto dirt, sand falls, a furnace burns down. §12 names them and M8 is
# where they arrive, because they are the first thing in this engine that
# **changes without being asked** — which §12.1 named as the trigger for the
# server tick, and is why that tick exists as of this milestone.
#
# ## Two mechanisms, because sampling cannot do falling
#
# §12 lists "run node timers, run due ABMs" as separate steps and the first
# version of this file collapsed them into one. It did not work, and the number
# says why: sampling 900 positions a pass out of 2.4M nodes takes about six
# minutes to visit one *particular* node, so a column of sand whose support was
# just dug stands there for minutes. A probe measured exactly that — the tick
# ran, and nothing fell.
#
# So there are two:
#
# - **The check queue** is targeted. An edit pushes the positions around it, the
#   tick drains them, and a node that falls pushes its own neighbours — which is
#   what makes a column cascade rather than settle one node per pass. This is
#   what upstream does for falling nodes and it is a *neighbour update*, not an
#   ABM.
# - **Sampling** is ambient, and is right for what it was designed for: grass
#   spreading onto a dirt block nobody touched. Nothing uses it yet, and it is
#   kept because it is §12's stated mechanism and the second ABM will want it.
#
# The lesson is worth keeping: "run it on random positions" and "run it where
# something just happened" look like the same feature and are not.
#
# ## Sampled, not exhaustive
#
# §12: "ABMs are sampled, not exhaustive — a block picks a random subset of
# positions per interval. This is upstream's approach and it is what keeps
# 'grass spreads' from being O(loaded world) every tick."
#
# This world is 2.4M nodes. At 5 Hz an exhaustive pass would be 12M node reads a
# second before any ABM did anything, which is not a budget question but a
# category error — the whole design is that a *few* positions get looked at
# often enough that the behaviour reads as continuous.
#
# So each pass samples `positions_per_pass` node positions, and an ABM fires on
# one when its group matches and its chance roll passes. A player watching sand
# fall sees it happen within a few hundred milliseconds; nobody can tell that
# the engine did not check every grain.
#
# ## The behaviour is a kind, not a callback — for now
#
# `register_abm` names one of a small set of engine-provided behaviours and
# parameterises it, exactly as `register_tile` names one of three procedural
# generators (§9.1). That is not where §9 wants to end up — it writes
# `^action (fn [pos node] …)` — and the reason it stops here is a compiler gap
# rather than a design choice: **the VM rejects a nominal type inside a
# `Callback` annotation**, so `(Callback [Game World F64 F64 F64] Nil)`
# compiles for the web profile and fails on the VM, which is the backend that
# would run it. §D7.17 records it.
#
# A kind is still a real mod-facing mechanism — the mod says which group falls
# and how often, and a mod adding its own falling node gets the behaviour by
# joining the group — but it is a fixed vocabulary, and a fixed vocabulary is
# the thing §9 exists to avoid.
#
# ## An action asks, it does not write
#
# An ABM action receives the world and a position and calls `queue_node`, which
# records the change rather than performing it. The server drains the queue
# after the pass and applies each one through §7.1's `apply_node` — the same
# path a player's dig takes, so lighting is recomputed and a delta reaches every
# client by the route that already works.
#
# **That indirection is the point rather than an inconvenience.** An action that
# wrote directly would have to be handed the lighting scratch buffers and the
# bounds tracker, which is the engine's working state and is exactly what §D5
# says mod code should not hold. Queueing keeps a mod's reach to "name a
# position and a node", which is a sentence the capability model can eventually
# police.

(mod abm ^profile web)

(let max_abms 64.0)
# One pass's worth of queued changes. A falling column enqueues two per node it
# moves, and a pass that filled this would be an ABM misbehaving rather than a
# world being busy — so it is a ceiling that says so instead of a buffer that
# grows.
(let max_queued 512.0)

# The behaviours the engine knows how to run. One so far.
(let abm_fall 0.0)     # a node with air below moves down one

(type AbmRegistry
  ^props {^labels    (List Str)
          ^kind      (Buffer F32)   # which engine behaviour
          ^group     (Buffer F32)   # the group a node must be in to match
          ^interval  (Buffer F32)   # milliseconds between passes for this ABM
          ^chance    (Buffer F32)   # 1 in N per sampled position
          ^due       (Buffer F32)   # monotonic ms when this ABM next runs
          # The queue an action writes into: position and the node to become.
          ^queue_x   (Buffer F32)
          ^queue_y   (Buffer F32)
          ^queue_z   (Buffer F32)
          ^queue_id  (Buffer F32)
          # Positions to look at next tick, seeded by whatever just changed.
          ^check_x   (Buffer F32)
          ^check_y   (Buffer F32)
          ^check_z   (Buffer F32)
          ^counts    (Buffer F32)}  # abms, queued edits, pending checks

  (ctor []
    (set! self/labels [])
    (set! self/kind ($buffer F32 max_abms))
    (set! self/group ($buffer F32 max_abms))
    (set! self/interval ($buffer F32 max_abms))
    (set! self/chance ($buffer F32 max_abms))
    (set! self/due ($buffer F32 max_abms))
    (set! self/queue_x ($buffer F32 max_queued))
    (set! self/queue_y ($buffer F32 max_queued))
    (set! self/queue_z ($buffer F32 max_queued))
    (set! self/queue_id ($buffer F32 max_queued))
    (set! self/check_x ($buffer F32 max_queued))
    (set! self/check_y ($buffer F32 max_queued))
    (set! self/check_z ($buffer F32 max_queued))
    (set! self/counts ($buffer F32 3.0))))

(let count_abms 0.0)
(let count_queued 1.0)
(let count_checks 2.0)

(fn new_abms [] : AbmRegistry
  (new AbmRegistry))

(fn abm_count [a : AbmRegistry] : F64 (a/counts ~ get count_abms))
(fn queued_count [a : AbmRegistry] : F64 (a/counts ~ get count_queued))

(type AbmRegistryFull
  ^props {^message Str}
  (ctor [message : Str] (set! self/message message)))

(impl Error for AbmRegistryFull)

# --- registration ------------------------------------------------------------

(fn add_abm [a : AbmRegistry label : Str kind : F64 group : F64
             interval_ms : F64 chance : F64] : F64
  (var id (abm_count a))
  (if_yes (>= id max_abms)
    (fail (new AbmRegistryFull $"abm registry is full at ${max_abms} entries")))
  (a/labels ~ push! label)
  (a/kind ~ set! id kind)
  (a/group ~ set! id group)
  (a/interval ~ set! id interval_ms)
  (a/chance ~ set! id chance)
  (a/due ~ set! id 0.0)
  (a/counts ~ set! count_abms (+ id 1.0))
  id)

(fn abm_label [a : AbmRegistry id : F64] : Str
  (var l a/labels)
  (var i id)
  l/%i)

(fn abm_kind [a : AbmRegistry id : F64] : F64 (a/kind ~ get id))
(fn abm_group [a : AbmRegistry id : F64] : F64 (a/group ~ get id))
(fn abm_interval [a : AbmRegistry id : F64] : F64 (a/interval ~ get id))
(fn abm_chance [a : AbmRegistry id : F64] : F64 (a/chance ~ get id))
(fn abm_due [a : AbmRegistry id : F64] : F64 (a/due ~ get id))
(fn set_abm_due [a : AbmRegistry id : F64 at : F64] : Nil
  (a/due ~ set! id at))

# --- the queue ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# What an action calls. Silently dropping past the ceiling would make a busy
# world quietly lose changes, so a full queue is a refusal the caller can see —
# `queue_node` answers whether it took the change.

(fn queue_node [a : AbmRegistry x : F64 y : F64 z : F64 id : F64] : Bool
  (var n (queued_count a))
  (if (>= n max_queued) false
    (do
      (a/queue_x ~ set! n x)
      (a/queue_y ~ set! n y)
      (a/queue_z ~ set! n z)
      (a/queue_id ~ set! n id)
      (a/counts ~ set! count_queued (+ n 1.0))
      true)))

(fn queued_x [a : AbmRegistry i : F64] : F64 (a/queue_x ~ get i))
(fn queued_y [a : AbmRegistry i : F64] : F64 (a/queue_y ~ get i))
(fn queued_z [a : AbmRegistry i : F64] : F64 (a/queue_z ~ get i))
(fn queued_id [a : AbmRegistry i : F64] : F64 (a/queue_id ~ get i))

(fn clear_queue [a : AbmRegistry] : Nil
  (a/counts ~ set! count_queued 0.0))

# --- the check queue ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# What an edit seeds and the tick drains. Bounded like the edit queue, and for
# the same reason: a cascade that filled it is a runaway rather than a busy
# world, and dropping the overflow makes it settle instead of grow.

(fn check_count [a : AbmRegistry] : F64 (a/counts ~ get count_checks))

(fn queue_check [a : AbmRegistry x : F64 y : F64 z : F64] : Bool
  (var n (check_count a))
  (if (>= n max_queued) false
    (do
      (a/check_x ~ set! n x)
      (a/check_y ~ set! n y)
      (a/check_z ~ set! n z)
      (a/counts ~ set! count_checks (+ n 1.0))
      true)))

(fn check_x_at [a : AbmRegistry i : F64] : F64 (a/check_x ~ get i))
(fn check_y_at [a : AbmRegistry i : F64] : F64 (a/check_y ~ get i))
(fn check_z_at [a : AbmRegistry i : F64] : F64 (a/check_z ~ get i))

(fn clear_checks [a : AbmRegistry] : Nil
  (a/counts ~ set! count_checks 0.0))

# --- sampling ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A deterministic walk rather than a random one, and the reason is §D3.1's
# discipline rather than reproducibility for its own sake: a spec that runs on
# two backends cannot share a generator, and an ABM that fired on different
# positions per backend could not be tested at all. The walk visits positions
# far apart in all three axes so consecutive samples are unrelated, which is the
# only property randomness was buying here.
#
# The multipliers are coprime with the world extent so the walk does not close
# into a short cycle.

(let stride_x 97.0)
(let stride_y 43.0)
(let stride_z 61.0)

(fn sample_x [step : F64 span : F64] : F64
  (var v (* step stride_x))
  (- v (* span ($math/floor (/ v span)))))

(fn sample_y [step : F64 span : F64] : F64
  (var v (* step stride_y))
  (- v (* span ($math/floor (/ v span)))))

(fn sample_z [step : F64 span : F64] : F64
  (var v (* step stride_z))
  (- v (* span ($math/floor (/ v span)))))

# 1-in-`chance`, from the same walk. `chance` of 1 always fires, which is what a
# falling node wants — sand does not hesitate.
(fn chance_passes? [step : F64 chance : F64] : Bool
  (if (<= chance 1.0) true
    (== 0.0 (- (/ step chance) ($math/floor (/ step chance))))))
